\title{Pre-Extracted IEMOCAP Features Have Seen Four of Five Test Folds:\\
A Leakage Audit of Widely Reused Multimodal Emotion Features}

\author{\IEEEauthorblockN{Anonymous Authors}
\IEEEauthorblockA{Affiliation withheld for double-blind review}}

\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
Research on multimodal emotion recognition in conversation often reuses pre-extracted
utterance features instead of raw recordings. We show that among the most widely reused
IEMOCAP features, the text and visual blocks carry the labels of the sessions most evaluation protocols test on. Their text
and visual extractors were trained on sessions 1--4, a fact the original authors
disclosed but whose consequence has not, to our knowledge, been drawn: any protocol that
tests on those sessions evaluates a model on data its features were fitted to. With one
fixed model and three seeds, holding out session 4 instead of session 5 raises text-only
accuracy by 31 points; replacing the text features with any of three frozen encoders that
were never trained on IEMOCAP labels reverses the gap to about $-7$ points. Under five-fold
leave-one-session-out cross-validation the released features report 83.6\% accuracy
against 57.6\% on the standard split, while frozen features show no inflation. The same
text features are shipped, value for value, in the DialogueGCN and MMGCN feature files;
four published systems we verified load them, and a fifth loads a bc-LSTM-derived file.
None of these has yet used a cross-validation protocol, so the leak is a latent hazard
rather than an error in published results --- but fusing clean text with the
contaminated visual block already yields a misleading finding on the standard split. We recommend a session-gap diagnostic and the
standard split for anyone using these files.
\end{abstract}

\begin{IEEEkeywords}
emotion recognition in conversation, data leakage, evaluation protocol, IEMOCAP,
multimodal features, reproducibility
\end{IEEEkeywords}

%------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
\section{Introduction}

Multimodal emotion recognition in conversation (MERC) has converged on a small set of
corpora and, for IEMOCAP~\cite{busso2008}, on a small set of pre-extracted feature files.
Most systems load utterance-level vectors distributed with earlier
work~\cite{poria2017,ghosal2019,hu2021mmgcn} instead of processing raw audio and video.
The practice lowers the cost of entry and keeps systems comparable; it also makes the
provenance of those files part of every result built on them.

Among the most reused IEMOCAP features are those introduced with
bc-LSTM~\cite{poria2017}. Their
text and visual vectors are hidden activations of networks trained on the emotion labels
of the training split, sessions 1--4; the audio vectors are unsupervised acoustic
descriptors. The original authors state this openly, and for the standard split, which
tests on session 5, it is harmless. IEMOCAP, however, has no agreed test set: papers also
report leave-one-session-out and speaker-independent cross-validation, and a recent audit
recommends cross-validation~\cite{antoniou2023}. Under those protocols four of five test
folds are sessions the feature extractors were fitted to. We hypothesise that the released
features therefore carry test labels into any protocol that tests on sessions 1--4, and
that the effect is large enough to change conclusions. The hypothesis can fail, and it can
be tested with the feature files alone: a clean feature block shows no advantage on
session 4 over session 5.

This matters because pre-extracted files are the entry point to MERC for groups without
the resources to process raw recordings, so their validity bounds the validity of the
comparisons built on them; five of the twenty recent systems we surveyed load the shared
text block (Section~\ref{sec:prov}). A leak in them cannot be seen in any results table,
surfaces whenever a protocol changes, and, as we show, can bias analyses even on the
standard split when released and clean features are combined.

In this paper, we audit these features. Our contributions are:
\begin{itemize}
\item a per-channel measurement and a control with label-free encoders: holding out
  session 4 instead of session 5 favours the released text by 31 points, and the gap
  reverses to about $-7$ points when three frozen encoders never trained on IEMOCAP labels
  replace it (Section~\ref{sec:gap});
\item a measurement of leave-one-session-out inflation: $+24$ to $+31$ points of accuracy
  on the released features and none on clean ones, across a sequence model and a linear
  probe (Section~\ref{sec:loso});
\item a provenance and usage analysis: the DialogueGCN and MMGCN files ship the identical
  text block, which five published systems load, none of them yet under
  cross-validation (Section~\ref{sec:prov});
\item two consequences outside cross-validation, on IEMOCAP's standard split and on
  MOSI/MOSEI: a misleading fusion finding and saturated model selection
  (Section~\ref{sec:conseq});
\item a session-gap diagnostic that needs only the feature files
  (Section~\ref{sec:rec}).
\end{itemize}
We do not claim that any published standard-split result is wrong.

%------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
\section{Related Work}

\textbf{Data leakage.} Information from the evaluation data reaching the model during
training is a recognised cause of irreproducible results across machine-learning-based
science~\cite{kapoor2023}. In neuroimaging, leakage through feature selection has been
shown to inflate prediction performance drastically~\cite{rosenblatt2024}, and in NLP the
analogous concern is benchmark contamination of large language models~\cite{balloccu2024}. A common form is preprocessing fitted on the full dataset
before splitting; pre-extracted features trained on a fixed split and later reused under
a different split are a variant of it that is invisible to the user of the files.

\textbf{IEMOCAP evaluation.} IEMOCAP lacks an agreed test set, and papers report
speaker-dependent splits, session-based splits and five- or ten-fold cross-validation,
often with different label sets~\cite{antoniou2023}. Recent toolkits ship fixed
partitions to counter inconsistent splits~\cite{ma2024emobox}, and a recent critique of
MERC evaluation identifies inconsistent feature sets, tuning on the test set and seed
selection as sources of unreliable comparisons~\cite{tu2026}. None of these addresses how
the shared features were produced.

\textbf{Feature reuse in MERC.} Pre-extracted features have become the common substrate of
conversational models: the bc-LSTM features~\cite{poria2017} are loaded by the DialogueRNN
and DialogueGCN releases~\cite{majumder2019,ghosal2019}, and MMGCN~\cite{hu2021mmgcn}
distributes a file that later models reuse in turn; a recent survey reviews the families of
MERC models built this way and how they are evaluated~\cite{wu2025merc}. These releases
document how the
features were extracted but leave open which evaluation protocols the extraction makes
unsafe; the one cross-validated evaluation we found with the same extraction
pipeline~\cite{poria2018baselines} states neither whether it used these files nor whether
the extractors were retrained per fold. We supply the missing link between provenance and protocol, and measure what it
costs.

%------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
\section{Experimental Setup}\label{sec:setup}

\textbf{Data.} IEMOCAP~\cite{busso2008} contains 151 dyadic dialogues in five sessions,
each session performed by a different pair of actors. The released feature file covers
7{,}433 utterances with six emotion labels (happy, sad, neutral, angry, excited,
frustrated), split into 120 training dialogues (sessions 1--4) and 31 test dialogues
(session 5). It provides 100-d text, 100-d audio and 512-d visual vectors per utterance.

\textbf{How the released features were built.} Poria et al.~\cite{poria2017} describe a
text network that applies convolutions to word2vec embeddings and a visual 3D
convolutional network, each topped by a fully connected layer and a softmax over the
emotion labels and trained on the training split; the activations of the fully connected
layer serve as the utterance features. The audio vectors are openSMILE acoustic
descriptors, computed without labels. We call a block \emph{label-fit} if its extractor
was trained on IEMOCAP emotion labels. The released file differs in detail from that
description --- its text and visual vectors have 100 and 512 dimensions, and it carries
six emotion classes where the paper reports four --- so the exact configuration that
produced it is not documented. What the diagnostic below tests does not depend on those
details: whether a block behaves as if it had seen the labels of the sessions it is
evaluated on.

\textbf{Frozen encoders.} To obtain text features that were never trained on IEMOCAP
labels, we encoded the transcripts shipped in the same file with BERT-base~\cite{devlin2019},
RoBERTa-base~\cite{liu2019roberta} and DeBERTa-v3-base~\cite{he2023debertav3}, frozen and
never fine-tuned, taking the mean of the last-layer states (half precision, one 6\,GB GPU).

\textbf{Model.} All experiments use one model: a bidirectional LSTM
(bi-LSTM)~\cite{hochreiter1997} over the utterances of a dialogue with a per-utterance linear read-out (hidden size 100,
dropout 0.5, Adam with learning rate $10^{-3}$, up to 50 epochs with early stopping on a
seeded 20\% of the training dialogues), trained with three seeds (five in
Section~\ref{sec:conseq}). Features are
standardised with training statistics. Only the input features change between runs.

\textbf{Protocols.} \emph{Standard}: train on sessions 1--4, test on session 5.
\emph{Session-4 hold-out}: train on sessions 1--3, test on session 4; both test sets are
unseen by the model, but only session 5 is unseen by the feature extractors.
\emph{Leave-one-session-out (LOSO)}: five folds, each session tested once by a model
trained on the other four; fold 5 equals the standard split, and the LOSO mean is the
unweighted mean over folds.

