\section{The Session Gap}\label{sec:gap}

Let $a_4$ be the accuracy of a model trained on sessions 1--3 and tested on session 4,
and $a_5$ that of the same model trained on sessions 1--4 and tested on session 5; we call
$g=a_4-a_5$ the \emph{session gap}. If a feature block was fitted to the labels of
sessions 1--4, a model reading it will look far better on session 4 than on session 5,
and a large positive $g$ will appear only in the label-fit blocks. Because $a_4$ comes
from three training sessions and $a_5$ from four, $g$ is biased downward for every block,
which works against this prediction. Table~\ref{tab:gap} shows the pattern.

