The released text features give 90.45\% on session 4 and 59.36\% on session 5, a gap of
31 points. Unsupervised audio moves by six points. When the text block is replaced by any of
three frozen encoders, the gap does not merely shrink; it reverses to between $-7.1$ and
$-7.7$, consistently across encoders. On clean features session 4 is the harder session,
which is unremarkable for two sessions performed by different actors. The frozen
encoders also yield 2.3 to 5.2 points higher accuracy than the released text features on
the standard split, so
the released text features are both contaminated under other protocols and weaker under
the one they were built for.

\textbf{Is it label shift?} Sessions do differ in class balance (Table~\ref{tab:sessions}):
the largest class covers 31.8\% of session 4 and 23.7\% of session 5. An 8-point
difference in majority-class share is unlikely to explain a 31-point gap, and it points the
wrong way for the clean features, which score \emph{lower} on session 4. The gap is
consistent with what the features were fitted to, not with what the sessions contain.

