\section{Downstream Consequences}\label{sec:conseq}

\textbf{A misleading fusion finding.} Contaminated features can mislead even under the
standard split when they are combined with clean ones. With frozen BERT text, adding the
released audio \emph{and} visual blocks \emph{lowers} accuracy by 3.4 points in a model
without conversational context and by 6.5 points in one with context (five seeds, paired
$t$-test, $p\le0.003$), which reads as evidence that extra modalities hurt once context is
available. Separating the blocks dissolves that reading: adding audio alone
\emph{raises} accuracy by 1.2 and 1.1 points, while adding the visual block alone lowers
it by 6.2 and 8.6 (Table~\ref{tab:trap}). A plausible mechanism is that the model relies
on a visual block fitted to sessions 1--4, which does not transfer to session 5. The
fused numbers alone support the wrong conclusion.

