\section{Discussion}

\textbf{How a disclosed choice became a hazard.} The original extraction was sound for its
purpose: a fixed split, extractors fitted on its training part, features released so that
others could replicate the model. The hazard came from two later drifts. The files were
reused as generic features by releases that did not restate their provenance, and
evaluation practice moved toward cross-validation, which the files were never built for.
Neither drift is visible in a results table, and the one place the provenance is written,
the original paper, is rarely read by the users of a downstream file. In the terms of
Kapoor and Narayanan~\cite{kapoor2023}, it is leakage from preprocessing fitted on data
that later serves as test data, distributed as a file rather than as code.

\textbf{What the evidence licenses.} Standard-split results on these files remain valid,
and our survey found no published cross-validated result on them. We therefore claim a
hazard and its size, not a list of wrong papers. The size is what makes the hazard
matter: under LOSO the released features inflated accuracy by 19 to 26 points for the
models we tested, which would swamp the one- or two-point margins on which a
new system is usually preferred.

\textbf{Clean features are now cheap.} The frozen encoders we used run inference-only on a
6\,GB consumer GPU, and on the standard split they yield 2.3 to 5.2 points higher
accuracy than the released text features. The economic reason to reuse supervised 2017-era features has
largely disappeared, while the validity reason against them has become measurable.

\textbf{Provenance as metadata.} A feature release can state in one line which utterances
its extractors were fitted to. That single field would have made the hazard visible to
downstream users, and we recommend it for any shared feature file, in the spirit of
datasheets for datasets~\cite{gebru2021datasheets}.

%------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
\section{Recommendations}\label{sec:rec}

For anyone evaluating on these feature files we recommend four practices, complementing
general checklists such as REFORMS~\cite{kapoor2024reforms}.
\begin{enumerate}
\item \textbf{State the protocol and use the standard split.} Results on these files are
  valid only when the test data were unseen by the feature extractors: train on sessions
  1--4, test on session 5.
\item \textbf{Run the session-gap diagnostic} on any pre-extracted feature block before
  cross-validating (Table~\ref{tab:diag}). A gap far above that of an unsupervised block
  signals supervised extraction.
\item \textbf{Report held-out beside test accuracy.} Near-perfect held-out accuracy with
  ordinary test accuracy is the visible signature of contaminated features.
\item \textbf{Re-extract with frozen encoders} when cross-validation is required. Frozen,
  inference-only encoders were never trained on benchmark labels, run on a consumer GPU,
  and in our measurements yield higher accuracy than the released text features.
\end{enumerate}

