\section{Limitations}

We re-extracted only the text channel of IEMOCAP; re-extracting video requires the raw
recordings. The visual contamination is shown by its session gap and its effect on
fusion, not by a clean replacement. We tested two readers, a bi-LSTM and a linear probe; a model
that relies less on the text block would be affected less. Which label set trained the released extractors
(four or six classes) is not documented, which does not change the diagnosis but limits
how exactly the leak can be characterised. For MOSI and MOSEI we show saturation, not a
per-session test, because their splits are not organised by session.

%------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
\section{Conclusion}

The text and visual blocks of some of the most reused IEMOCAP features were fitted to the
labels of four of its five sessions. That was a reasonable design for the protocol they
were built for and a hazard for the protocols that came after. The hazard is invisible in
a results table, inflated accuracy by 19 to 26 points under LOSO for the models we tested,
and can produce misleading findings even on the standard split when contaminated and
clean features are mixed. A session-gap check
takes minutes and should precede any cross-validated claim on shared features.

